Nutritional parameters of feed substrates and BSFL
The dry matter content differed significantly between the diets, with PP containing up to 3.8-fold more water than the others (Welch’s F(3, 4.12) =2472.24; P < 0.00001; Table 2). CF
contained more than twice as much crude ash as the agro-industrial side streams (Welch’s F(3, 3.48) =48.38; P = 0.005). The crude fiber content was highest in EFB and CPC and
differed significantly by more than 35% from CF and PP (F(3, 8) =1868.18; P < 0.00001).
The EFB and CPC diets had the greatest crude fat content, differing significantly from CF and PP, the latter containing only 0.2% DM of fat (Welch’s F(3, 3.89) =475.33; P = 0.00001).
Total nitrogen (F(3, 8) =1491.73; P < 0.00001) and crude protein calculated from it (Welch’s
F(3, 4.77) =1039.51; P < 0.00001) differed significantly across the diets. CPC was the most
protein-rich, followed by CF with 8.0% less protein. CF and CPC resulted in a significantly higher larval dry matter content than EFB and PP (Welch’s F(3, 3.40) =1140.69; P = 0.00004; Table 2). However, the crude ash content of
larvae reared on CF and CPC was significantly lower than that of larvae on the other diets (F(3, 9) =114.87; P < 0.00001). All diets affected the larval crude fat content, and larvae
in the CF group were able to build up the most fat, even though the fat content of the diet was only 2.5% DM (F(3, 8) =110.94; P < 0.00001). Concomitantly, despite the very low
crude fat content of the diet, PP larvae accumulated an 84-fold higher amount of fat in their biomass. Total nitrogen and crude protein (Welch’s F(3, 4.36) =70.43; P = 0.0006) levels
were significantly (1.2-fold) higher in larvae reared on CPC and PP compared with CF. We also clarified the extent to which the dietary nutritional parameters of crude fat, protein, and fiber were linearly related to BSF life-history traits. Critical temporal
